WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.027
Welcome to Lecture 9 of Machine Learning.

00:00:03.027 --> 00:00:08.747
Our question is how to learn useful features rather than choose every feature by hand.

00:00:08.747 --> 00:00:14.067
In logistic regression, we apply a sigmoid to a weighted sum of an input vector.

00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:19.587
In a support vector machine, we choose a separating boundary, often using a kernel.

00:00:19.587 --> 00:00:24.110
Both approaches depend on how the original examples are represented.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:28.300
A poor representation can make even a good classifier struggle.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.253
Imagine classifying handwritten digits.

00:00:31.253 --> 00:00:34.858
Raw pixels tell us brightness at individual positions.

00:00:34.858 --> 00:00:42.000
Features such as strokes and loops would be more directly useful, but designing them manually is difficult.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.543
A neural network learns intermediate quantities from the pixels, then uses those quantities to predict the label.

00:00:49.543 --> 00:00:53.414
We still specify the objective and optimization procedure.

00:00:53.414 --> 00:01:00.690
What changes is that some of the feature transformation becomes trainable along with the final decision rule.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.658
This lecture has 3 parts.

00:01:02.658 --> 00:01:05.959
First, we build neurons and hidden representations.

00:01:05.959 --> 00:01:10.037
Second, we derive backpropagation through computational graphs.

00:01:10.037 --> 00:01:17.416
Third, we connect multilayer learning, expressive power, and computational cost to the motivation for convolution.

00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:27.902
By the end, you should be able to calculate a forward pass, explain why nonlinear activations matter, and derive gradients rather than memorize an update formula.

00:01:27.902 --> 00:01:34.440
Keep the distinction between representing a function and successfully learning it in mind throughout.

00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:42.802
The lecture motivates an artificial neuron with biological neurons receiving excitatory and inhibitory signals.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:46.892
This is an analogy, not an accurate model of brain function.

00:01:46.892 --> 00:01:54.665
Mathematically, a unit multiplies each input by a weight, adds the products and a bias, and applies an activation.

00:01:54.665 --> 00:02:00.392
A positive weight can increase the preactivation; a negative weight can decrease it.

00:02:00.392 --> 00:02:05.233
The bias shifts the response independently of the current input vector.

00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:08.385
Calculate one unit with 2 inputs.

00:02:08.385 --> 00:02:10.848
The input values are 1 and 2.

00:02:10.848 --> 00:02:14.754
The weights are 0.5 and -1, and the bias is 1.

00:02:14.754 --> 00:02:17.471
Multiplication gives 0.5 and -2.

00:02:17.471 --> 00:02:19.764
Adding the bias gives -0.5.

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:23.500
This is the preactivation, usually called z.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:30.038
Notice that a negative preactivation does not yet determine the final output.

00:02:30.038 --> 00:02:34.623
We must know which activation function the model uses.

00:02:34.973 --> 00:02:38.152
With a 0 threshold step, -0.5 produces 0.

00:02:38.152 --> 00:02:45.903
ReLU also returns 0 here, but for positive inputs it returns the input value rather than a binary 1.

00:02:45.903 --> 00:02:48.772
Sigmoid returns approximately 0.3775.

00:02:48.772 --> 00:02:52.957
These are different functions with different purposes.

00:02:52.957 --> 00:03:01.639
A perceptron commonly uses a hard decision; differentiable networks use activations suited to gradient training.

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:07.453
The input layer supplies values; it need not perform this same computation.

00:03:07.803 --> 00:03:14.080
Slide 11 gives a hard-sign model and then approximates its derivative by one for an update.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.977
This is a surrogate rule, not the true derivative of the sign function.

00:03:18.977 --> 00:03:22.632
The classical perceptron instead updates on mistakes.

00:03:22.632 --> 00:03:31.667
With labels plus or minus 1, add learning rate times target times input to the weights, and learning rate times target to the bias.

00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:35.943
A smooth sigmoid model supports ordinary loss differentiation.

00:03:35.943 --> 00:03:47.323
Keep these training rules distinct: replacing an inconvenient derivative by 1 is an additional modeling choice, not a mathematical consequence of the hard threshold.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:54.298
The slides compare linear, ReLU, softplus, sigmoid, and hyperbolic tangent activations.

00:03:54.298 --> 00:03:57.575
Softplus smoothly approximates the ReLU shape.

00:03:57.575 --> 00:04:04.058
Sigmoid has outputs between 0 and 1, while hyperbolic tangent has outputs between -1 and 1.

00:04:04.058 --> 00:04:09.900
Saturated sigmoid units have small derivatives, which will matter during learning.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:15.813
ReLU is not differentiable exactly at 0; implementations select a convention there.

00:04:15.813 --> 00:04:22.580
A linear activation does not introduce the nonlinearity we need for richer decision boundaries.

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.933
A perceptron can implement familiar logic.

00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:30.508
For binary inputs, threshold the sum minus 1.5 to implement AND.

00:04:30.508 --> 00:04:32.796
Only two 1s cross the threshold.

00:04:32.796 --> 00:04:35.941
Threshold the sum minus 0.5 to implement OR.

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:38.729
For NOT, threshold 0.5 minus the input.

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:43.304
We define the step to return 1 when its argument is nonnegative.

00:04:43.304 --> 00:04:50.810
None of these examples lands exactly on 0, so the boundary convention does not affect their truth tables.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.244
Now try exclusive OR, or XOR.

00:04:53.244 --> 00:04:55.399
Exactly one input should be 1.

00:04:55.399 --> 00:05:03.803
The positive points occupy opposite corners of the unit square, and the negative points occupy the other two corners.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:06.102
No straight line separates them.

00:05:06.102 --> 00:05:10.484
Therefore a single perceptron cannot represent this function.

00:05:10.484 --> 00:05:22.480
Read the slide's statement about simple Boolean gates with this restriction: perceptrons represent linearly separable Boolean functions, not all possible binary logic.

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:25.063
Introduce 2 hidden units.

00:05:25.063 --> 00:05:28.655
The first computes OR and the second computes AND.

00:05:28.655 --> 00:05:35.623
Use the first hidden output with weight 1, the second with weight -1, and an output bias of -0.5.

00:05:35.623 --> 00:05:40.579
This reproduces the structure of the XOR construction in the lecture.

00:05:40.579 --> 00:05:47.188
We explicitly use binary step outputs because sign functions sometimes use -1 and 1 instead.

00:05:47.188 --> 00:05:51.067
Mixing these conventions would change the calculation.

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:53.005
Trace the 4 inputs.

00:05:53.005 --> 00:05:56.764
0, 0 maps to hidden values 0, 0 and output 0.

00:05:56.764 --> 00:06:00.440
0, 1 and 1, 0 both map to 1, 0 and output 1.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.030
1, 1 maps to 1, 1 and output 0.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:08.377
In hidden coordinates, the final classes are linearly separable.

00:06:08.377 --> 00:06:14.141
A layer computes a function, so several layers compute a composition.

00:06:14.141 --> 00:06:17.065
This explains representation power.

00:06:17.065 --> 00:06:27.257
It does not prove that gradient descent can train hard step activations, whose derivatives are unhelpful for that purpose.

00:06:27.607 --> 00:06:31.898
For a 28 by 28 digit image, flattening gives 784 input values.

00:06:31.898 --> 00:06:39.510
A first-layer weight vector can be reshaped back into an image to inspect which pixel patterns it responds to.

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:42.625
The slide illustrates a stroke-like detector.

00:06:42.625 --> 00:06:50.029
Hidden units need not acquire human-readable concepts, but their activations form a learned representation.

00:06:50.029 --> 00:06:55.220
The output layer combines that representation to distinguish digit classes.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:03.247
Later, convolution will preserve spatial neighborhoods instead of fully connecting every pixel to every hidden unit.

00:07:03.597 --> 00:07:07.526
The next slide also gives a ReLU construction.

00:07:07.526 --> 00:07:10.345
Let the sum of the 2 inputs be s.

00:07:10.345 --> 00:07:14.445
Define hidden values as ReLU(s) and ReLU(s - 1).

00:07:14.445 --> 00:07:23.071
The output ReLU of the first hidden value minus twice the second gives the exact 0-1 XOR truth table.

00:07:23.071 --> 00:07:31.186
The displayed slide adds an extra -0.5 output bias, giving positive scores of 0.5 instead of 1.

00:07:31.186 --> 00:07:40.837
Those scores separate the classes, but a decision threshold is needed if the requested outputs are binary labels.

00:07:41.634 --> 00:07:47.520
Pause the video and calculate the hidden values and final prediction for input 1, 1.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.285
Then explain the result in words without using the term 'black box'.

00:07:52.285 --> 00:08:02.936
As a second question, predict whether adding 10 linear layers, with no nonlinear activation anywhere, can make a straight-line classifier represent XOR.

00:08:02.936 --> 00:08:05.880
Write down your answers before continuing.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:10.434
The aim is to reason about the computation, not recall a diagram.

00:08:10.984 --> 00:08:15.106
Both hidden units activate on 1, 1: OR is 1 and AND is 1.

00:08:15.106 --> 00:08:19.516
Their contributions cancel, and the output threshold gives 0.

00:08:19.516 --> 00:08:28.699
Adding linear or affine layers without nonlinear activations still produces a single affine function, so it does not solve XOR.

00:08:28.699 --> 00:08:34.266
Multiplying matrices changes the coefficients but not the class of functions.

00:08:34.266 --> 00:08:41.424
We will return to this fact when discussing what depth contributes and what it does not contribute.

00:08:41.774 --> 00:08:42.852
Remember 3 ideas.

00:08:42.852 --> 00:08:46.782
A mathematical neuron applies an activation to a weighted sum.

00:08:46.782 --> 00:08:52.868
Hidden layers can transform a difficult representation into one a final classifier can separate.

00:08:52.868 --> 00:08:58.066
Representing a function is different from finding its parameters through training.

00:08:58.066 --> 00:09:03.138
For practice, write all 4 rows of the XOR construction and check each threshold.

00:09:03.138 --> 00:09:10.174
In the next part, we keep the prediction smooth and work backward through its computation to learn the weights.

